# -text means: store and check out these bytes unchanged, on every platform.
# Git's autocrlf is on by default on Windows, and for these files a converted
# checkout is not cosmetic -- it changes what the file IS.

# The MATLAB reference files have a SHA-256 recorded in NOTICE, and that digest
# is over the bytes on disk. A CRLF checkout would fail every recorded checksum
# and make the provenance claim in NOTICE look false. (Measured: git's binary
# heuristic happens to spare these files today, so nothing was broken -- but
# nothing was guaranteeing it either. This states the requirement.)
matlab/*.m -text

# The generated engine and its provenance manifest. assemble_sicopr.py writes
# both with newline='\n' explicitly so that a Windows run and a Linux run
# produce identical bytes -- which is what lets CI diff them byte-for-byte
# against a fresh assembler run. That only holds if the checkout does not
# convert them back: without -text, autocrlf hands Windows a CRLF working copy,
# the assembler regenerates LF, and every regeneration shows as a whole-file
# diff. The two settings are a pair; changing one without the other breaks the
# generated-file check.
sicopr.py -text
com_functions/inlined_copies.json -text
